The '''Great Kaftian Range''' is a major mountain system extending approximately 5,331 km across southern [[Kaftia]], from the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast at [[Kijura]] and [[Fayeha]] in the southwest to the [[Kendriyan Ocean]] coast in eastern [[Desket]]. It is the longest mountain range on the Kaftian continent. The range forms the principal {{W|continental divide}} of [[Kaftia]], separating a tropical climatic zone to the north from a drier, more temperate zone to the south.

The highest point is '''[[Buur Waaq]]''' (2,300 m), located in the central [[Kushish]] section. The range is moderately old and extensively eroded, with rounded summits, broad valleys, and wide passes rather than jagged alpine peaks.

== Geography ==

=== Extent and orientation ===

The range runs roughly southwest to northeast across its full extent. In the [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southwestern Kaftian]] peninsula — which contains approximately half the range's total length — the range bifurcates into two arms that meet at the central [[Kushish]] highlands, forming a chevron shape open to the northwest:

* The '''southern arm''' runs from the coast of [[Kijura]] and [[Fayeha]] in the southwest through [[Burawa]] to the central [[Kushish]] highlands, following the main axis of the range.
* The '''northern arm''' branches northwest from the central highlands, forming the border between [[Burawa]] to the south and [[Dakare]] to the north before subsiding into the lowlands.

East of the vertex in the Kushish highlands, the range continues as a single chain through [[Kwalib]], along the [[Orma]]–[[Ayorin]] border, and into the continental section through [[Enfolo]] and [[Desket]], where it descends to the [[Kendriyan Ocean]].

The range varies considerably in width. It is narrowest in the southwest (approximately 100 km in [[Kijura]]) and widest at the central vertex through [[Kushish]] and [[Kwalib]] (up to 400 km), where the two arms converge into a broad highland plateau. East of [[Ayorin]], the range narrows again as it crosses [[Enfolo]] and enters [[Desket]].

=== Physiographic regions ===

The range is divided into five physiographic regions based on elevation, geology, and landscape character:

==== Kurinka Hills ====
The westernmost region extends from the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast in [[Kijura]] through [[Fayeha]]. The '''[[Kurinka Hills]]''' are a low ridge of 600–1,000 m — the lowest and narrowest portion of the range. The terrain consists of rounded, scrub-covered hills separated by shallow valleys. The hills form a barrier between [[Fayeha]] and the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] lowlands.

==== Dakare Spur ====
The northern arm of the range branches northwest from the Kushish Highlands, forming the border between [[Burawa]] and [[Dakare]]. This spur is lower and narrower than the main range, averaging 800–1,200 m, and subsides gradually into the northern savanna lowlands. The Dakare Spur separates the [[Dimar River|Dimar]] river basin from the broad plains of [[Dakare]] to the north.

==== Kushish Highlands ====
The central region, where the southern and northern arms of the range converge, passes through [[Burawa]], [[Kushish]], and [[Kwalib]]. This section is the widest (up to 400 km) and most elevated portion of the range. Peaks are rounded and eroded, reaching their maximum elevation at '''[[Buur Waaq]]''' (2,300 m) in central [[Kushish]]. The landscape is characterised by extensive highland plateaus between 1,200 and 1,800 m, deeply incised by river valleys that have cut through softer sedimentary layers to expose metamorphic bedrock below. The [[Lake Haro]] basin (8,850 km²) occupies a structural depression on the range's southern slopes in [[Burawa]].

The Kushish Highlands contain significant deposits of iron ore, copper, and tin (see [[#Mineral resources|Mineral resources]]).

==== Eastern Divide ====
East of [[Kwalib]], the range narrows and decreases in elevation, averaging 800–1,200 m. In this section the range forms the border between [[Orma]] to the south and [[Ayorin]] to the north. The terrain transitions from the open highland grassland of the Kushish Highlands to dense montane forest as rainfall increases near the continental junction. The southern slopes, facing [[Orma]] and [[Itagar]], are drier and more open; the northern slopes, facing [[Ayorin]], are wetter and merge into the fringes of the [[Ayorin Rainforest]].

==== Enfolo Plateau ====
East of the peninsula, the range rises in [[Enfolo]] to form a broad plateau at 1,000–1,500 m. Dense tropical jungle, a continuation of the [[Ayorin Rainforest]] system, covers the plateau. The combination of elevation and equatorial moisture produces high annual rainfall, feeding numerous rivers that drain north and south. Persistent cloud cover on the upper slopes supports a {{W|cloud forest}} ecosystem (see [[#Enfolo cloud forest|Enfolo cloud forest]]).

==== Desket Escarpment ====
At the eastern terminus, in [[Desket]], the range forms a steep {{W|escarpment}} dropping 1,000–1,500 m to the [[Kendriyan Ocean]] coastal plain. Cliff faces in this section are near-vertical in places. The escarpment summit receives heavy orographic rainfall from Kendriyan winds, while the narrow coastal strip below lies in the {{W|rain shadow}} of the range and is arid (see [[#Desket escarpment|Desket escarpment]]).

=== Climate ===

The range creates two distinct climatic zones across the Kaftian continent:

* '''North of the range''': Hotter, wetter conditions prevail. In the [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southwestern Kaftian]] peninsula, this zone comprises a broad tropical savanna with seasonal rainfall, dominated by the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] (107,000 km²) and the [[Dakare-Galwa Wetlands]]. The northeastern fringe transitions to dense tropical rainforest in [[Ayorin]].
* '''South of the range''': Drier, more temperate conditions, trending toward {{W|Mediterranean climate}} near the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast. Rainfall is concentrated in winter months. The southern slopes receive less moisture due to the {{W|rain shadow}} effect, and vegetation transitions from montane woodland on the upper slopes to dry scrubland and {{W|maquis}} on the lower hills.

The highlands themselves experience a {{W|subtropical highland climate}}, with cooler temperatures than the surrounding lowlands, occasional frost above 1,800 m, and significant diurnal temperature variation.

=== Principal passes ===

A number of passes allow movement between the northern and southern zones. The principal passes are concentrated in the Kushish Highlands, where deep river valleys cut natural corridors through the range.

* '''Jowhar Gap''': The most important pass in the range, located near [[Jowhar]] where the upper [[Buran River]] descends from the [[Kushish]] highlands. This is the principal corridor giving access to the highland interior from the lowlands. Elevation at the saddle: approximately 800 m.
* '''Kushish Gate''': A broad pass through the central [[Kushish]] highlands, connecting the northern savanna to the upper [[Buran River]] valley. The pass follows a wide, flat-bottomed valley between sandstone ridges.
* '''Orma Pass''': An eastern pass through the [[Orma]] highlands, serving as the principal route between the peninsula and the main [[Kaftia|Kaftian]] continent. Higher and narrower than the western passes, rising to approximately 1,400 m.
* '''Kurinka Saddle''': A low pass (~700 m) through the [[Kurinka Hills]], the only significant crossing point between [[Fayeha]] and the northern basin.

=== Prominence and topography ===

The highest point, [[Buur Waaq]], reaches 2,300 m. The surrounding lowlands on both sides lie near sea level — the northern savanna averages 100–300 m, the southern coastal plain 0–200 m — giving the range considerable {{W|topographic prominence}} relative to its surroundings. The central Kushish section rises 1,500–2,000 m above the adjacent plains.

The summits are characteristically rounded, a product of prolonged erosion. Ridgelines are broad and often flat-topped, with wide saddles between peaks. The valleys, by contrast, can be steep-sided where rivers have cut through softer rock layers, creating narrow gorges in the lower reaches of several major river systems.

== Geology ==

=== Formation ===

The Great Kaftian Mountain Range is a {{W|fold mountain}} system formed by the convergence of tectonic plates. The range is younger than the {{W|craton|cratonic}} shields of interior Kaftia but older than the volcanic systems of the [[Mesogean Sea]] basin. Prolonged erosion has reduced the peaks to their present rounded profiles.

=== Rock types ===

The dominant lithology varies across the range:

* '''Sedimentary''': {{W|Sandstone}} and {{W|limestone}} predominate in the western and central sections, forming the characteristic flat-topped ridges and stepped escarpments visible in [[Kushish]] and [[Kwalib]].
* '''Metamorphic''': {{W|Schist}} and {{W|gneiss}} are exposed in the deeply eroded valleys of the central range, where river cutting has penetrated below the sedimentary cap.
* '''Igneous intrusions''': Localised {{W|igneous rock|igneous}} intrusions occur in central [[Kushish]], producing deposits of {{W|obsidian}} (volcanic glass) and contributing to the concentration of metallic ores in this section.

=== Mineral resources ===

The central range, particularly the [[Kushish]] highlands, contains significant mineral deposits:

* '''Iron ore''': Abundant in surface and shallow deposits across [[Kushish]]. The principal ore type is {{W|hematite}}, occurring in exposed veins and weathered surface deposits.
* '''Copper''': Found in close proximity to iron deposits, particularly in eastern [[Kushish]] and western [[Kwalib]].
* '''Tin''': Localised deposits in [[Kwalib]] and eastern [[Kushish]], occurring as {{W|cassiterite}} in alluvial gravels and vein deposits. The proximity of tin to copper enabled indigenous {{W|bronze}} production.
* '''{{W|Obsidian}}''': Volcanic glass from igneous intrusions in central [[Kushish]]. Obsidian was among the earliest trade goods exchanged across the range.

The eastern and continental sections of the range are comparatively mineral-poor, with limited deposits of non-metallic minerals and building stone.

== Hydrology ==

The range serves as the principal {{W|drainage divide}} of the Kaftian continent. Rivers originating on the southern slopes drain into the [[Mesogean Sea]]; rivers on the northern slopes drain into the [[Great Kaftian Lake]], the [[Aethiopean Ocean]], or — in the continental section — the interior basins of northern Kaftia.

=== Southern drainage (Mesogean) ===

The southern slopes of the peninsular section give rise to the major river systems of southern [[Southwestern Kaftia]]:

* '''[[Buran River|Buran]]''' — rises in the [[Kushish]] highlands and splits into two branches in its lower course: one flowing into the [[Lake Haro]] system, the other continuing south to the [[Mesogean Sea]]; the peninsula's most economically significant river
* '''[[Galbeed River|Galbeed]]''' — a long southeastern river draining the eastern central range
* '''[[Dafar River|Dafar]]''' and '''[[Hooyal River|Hooyal]]''' — drain the western foothills and the agricultural lowlands of western [[Burawa]]
* '''[[Haroti River|Haroti]]''' — a short river near [[Lake Haro]]
* '''[[Badweyn River|Badweyn]]''' — drains far western [[Burawa]]
* '''[[Qorrax River|Qorrax]]''' — flows southwest to the coast

=== Northern drainage ===

The northern slopes drain into the broad savanna lowlands:

* '''[[Maayo Weela]]''' — flows north through [[Dakare]], rising in the central range
* '''[[Dimar River|Dimar]]''' — flows southeast from the [[Great Kaftian Lake]]
* '''[[Dex Tuuba]]''', '''[[Kitale River|Kitale]]''', '''[[Ndoxeel River|Ndoxeel]]''' — shorter rivers draining the western and northern slopes to the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]

=== Lakes ===

Two major lake systems are associated with the range:

* '''[[Lake Haro]]''' (8,850 km²): Sits in a structural basin on the southern slopes of the eastern range, primarily in [[Burawa]] with borders in [[Maianka]] and [[Sabia]]. Drains south to the [[Mesogean Sea]] via the [[Buran River]].
* '''[[Great Kaftian Lake]]''' (107,000 km²): Located in eastern [[Galwa]], fed by rivers draining the northern slopes. Drains northwest to the [[Aethiopean Ocean]].

== Ecology ==

=== Altitudinal zonation ===

The range supports a series of distinct ecological zones determined by elevation, rainfall, and aspect (north-facing vs. south-facing slopes):

==== Lowland foothills (below 500 m) ====

On the southern side, the foothills are characterised by dry {{W|maquis}} scrubland — the '''[[Burawi Maquis]]''' — composed of drought-adapted shrubs, aromatic herbs, and scattered {{W|juniper}} and {{W|wild olive}} trees. On the northern side, the foothills transition into tropical savanna grassland with scattered {{W|Acacia}} and {{W|baobab}} trees.

==== Montane zone (500–1,500 m) ====

The montane zone supports open woodland and grassland, with species composition varying by aspect. North-facing slopes receive more moisture and carry denser tree cover, including species of {{W|Podocarpus}} and {{W|Juniperus}}. South-facing slopes are drier and more open. This zone supports the range's largest mammal populations, including '''{{W|Common eland|giant eland}}''', '''{{W|Klipspringer|rock antelope}}''' (''klipspringer''), '''{{W|Mountain nyala}}''', and '''{{W|Leopard|Kaftian leopard}}'''.

==== Highland zone (1,500–2,000 m) ====

Above 1,500 m, tree cover thins and gives way to {{W|Afroalpine}} grassland — a landscape of tussock grasses, {{W|Lobelia}} stands, and scattered {{W|Erica}} (heather) shrubs. The '''{{W|Gelada|highland baboon}}''' — a Kaftian endemic — is restricted to this zone, living in large troops on the cliff faces and grasslands of the central range.

==== Summit zone (above 2,000 m) ====

The summit zone, found only in the central [[Kushish]] section near [[Buur Waaq]], supports sparse alpine-type vegetation: low grasses, mosses, lichens, and the distinctive '''giant {{W|Lobelia}}''' that grows in sheltered hollows. Temperatures drop below freezing on winter nights. Fauna is limited to raptors, small rodents, and occasional visits by highland baboon troops.

=== Biogeographic barrier ===

The range functions as a {{W|biogeographic barrier}}, limiting species dispersal between the northern tropical zone and the southern temperate zone. Many plant and animal species are restricted to one side of the range; the mountain passes serve as corridors for generalist species.

The isolated highland zone supports a number of {{W|endemic}} species. The highland baboon, several montane rodent species, and endemic plants in the ''Lobelia'' and ''Erica'' genera are restricted to the central range.

=== Eastern ecological transition ===

In [[Ayorin]], the montane ecology transitions to dense tropical {{W|montane forest}} as the [[Ayorin Rainforest]] climbs the lower slopes. This section has the highest biodiversity of any part of the range, with forest-dwelling primates, hornbills, and epiphytic plant communities absent from the drier western sections.

=== Enfolo cloud forest ===

The [[Enfolo]] plateau supports a {{W|cloud forest}} ecosystem. Persistent mist and cloud cover on the upper slopes sustain a canopy of moss-draped trees, tree ferns, and epiphytic orchids. The cloud forest receives high annual rainfall and contains a significant number of endemic species.

=== Desket escarpment ===

The [[Desket]] escarpment presents a steep ecological gradient. The summit receives heavy orographic rainfall and supports dense vegetation; the narrow coastal strip below lies in the rain shadow and is arid, supporting drought-adapted scrub and succulent plants. The cliff faces are largely bare rock, colonised by lichens and cliff-nesting raptors.

